-- Version 35 → 36: a session can say it was minted without proof.
--
-- Owner impersonation stopped being a costume. The owner is signed out, hands back
-- a short-lived grant, and is then signed in FOR REAL as any address they name —
-- the genuine sign-in pipeline, admission gate included, minus only the Google
-- exchange or the mailed code. Everything about the resulting session is ordinary
-- except how it came to exist, and this column is the only place that fact is
-- recorded.
--
-- ── A label, and why that is safe to write down ─────────────────────────────
--
-- The grant this names lives in the server's memory and nowhere else, so the value
-- stored here resolves to nothing outside one running process — a leaked copy of
-- this table is a list of ids that unlock no doors. The capability is deliberately
-- not stored beside the label, which is what keeps `sessions` no more sensitive
-- than it was before this column existed. See ProbeGrants.
--
-- ── NULL is every row, and stays that way on almost every deployment ────────
--
-- The feature is off unless LUNICLE_ENABLE_OWNER_IMPERSONATION says otherwise, so
-- on a normal instance nothing ever writes a non-NULL value here. Every row that
-- exists when this runs was minted against a real proof of identity — there was no
-- other way to make one — so NULL is not a backfill guess, it is the truth about
-- all of them.
ALTER TABLE sessions ADD COLUMN probe_id TEXT;
